A||||42||blue000||depression venting||||9||
Z||000000||blue000||12-20-2001||04:36 PM||||I was diagnosed with clinical depression, put on paxil and <BR>for a while did ok after a big crisis. I found the side effects to <BR>much and a friend recommended pot as an alternative, and for 1 1/2 <BR>years it worked pretty good with low incidents of depression or <BR>anxiety. I meet the love of my life last Christmas and in June we <BR>married. I then quit my job and we relocated to Chicago. Due to the <BR>need to pass a drug test, I stopped using pot and didn't use anything <BR>for depression. Once I got to Chicago, there was a terrorist attack that closed the job market in my field and no opportunity to find a job in Chicago. Things started to go bad a few weeks after I stopped <BR>taking anything for depression, the same feelings of the past came <BR>back, I started to go to a deep major depression and at the time I <BR>thought it was all the change and didn't want to admit to my self that <BR>I was going back to how things used to be in the past with clinical <BR>depression and suicide. Things in Chicago didn't go well in any way. <BR>I didn't find a job for 3 months, we where living with my wife's <BR>friends, and she was and still is financially supporting me. Our <BR>relationship went south I left Chicago about one month ago, We are <BR>still talking and I'm due to visit her in Chicago for new year. My <BR>problem is that I feel paralyzed here, I don't have any motivation, I <BR>spend my days sobbing, with high anxiety, depress, and I don't seem <BR>to be able to do anything. It seems easy just to end it all. The <BR>thing is that I would not want to destroy my family, by having such a <BR>reminder during Christmas for the rest of there lives.I know that it <BR>is all in my head, I don't have insurance so I went to Mexico and <BR>purchased paxil once again, I've been taking it for the last week. I <BR>know that I have to make efforts to get out of the house and look for <BR>work, and eat, and exercise etc etc. but I just don't seem to care <BR>about anything. I can see what I'm doing, but I don't seem to be able <BR>to change it. I feel that I'm just a lazy **** up. And I wish I could <BR>just die. I don't have any friends around and I will be spending <BR>Christmas alone, I know that no one can fix things for me, and I <BR>don't know what would be gained by talking to some one.<P><BR>Thanks for letting me vent, I just don't know what else to do for now. Just so you know my name here was funguy. Please keep the judgement to yourself. <P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Fleabitdog||12-20-2001||04:54 PM||||Welcome to the forum. I am sorry to hear of your troubles. I know this is a very bad time of the year also and that just makes matters more exaggerated. I know something about depression. I have been on and off anti depressants for about the past 28 years.<BR>I know it makes a big difference if you are not taking them when you should. I also know they are expensive and there are times that you just don't have the money or insurance to get them. When I have not taken my meds for 3 days I can see and feel the difference. Are you a veteran? I hope so. I can get my zoloft ( prefer prozac but have to go with what the doc decides) at the va for 2.00 a month. It is also very important to find a support group and or counseling. Try a mental health clinic. At least try to find a person you can talk about your feelings with. I hope things work out for you. My email addy is here if you ever want to talk or you can im me. Dave||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fleabitdog||12-20-2001||05:22 PM||||Blue<BR> also make it past xmas day. Things will get better. Hell I have spent every christmas sice 76 bymyself. But that is by my own choosing. I try to just treat it like another day and make it pass as quick as possible. Suicide I have tried quite a few times in my younger days. But only one time did i really want to die. 17 tuinols, I fell right out. Had to shock my heart. The other times I just wanted someone to love me.Poor me. Then came the drinking and drugging. Nothing helped. Until one day I hit my bottom. I had to choose between life and death. I chose life. I quit the trying to medicate myself. I asked for help and was sincere. It was there. That was a little over 14 years ago. Now today, my life is not the best. Still no wife or kids or steady job. Still feel like a worthless piece of crap at times. But it is still 1000% better than what it was. I guess I am just rambling on, but don't waste the years like I did. Get some help. The sooner you do it the sonner your life will get better. Things that seemed awful big before will seem a lot smaller in time. If you need someone to talk to I will be here. (at least til they cancel my aol service ha ha) Hopefully that won't happen. Take care and God Bless Dave ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||12-20-2001||05:35 PM||||Hey Blue, long time no talk to, well at least under your alternate moniker way back when and not knowing who you really are elsewise here on the forum.  <P>Judgements of any kind aside from me if you felt I had them against you at the time we last interacted.  I do agree with Fleabitdog.<P>Nough said at this time.  Don't know what to say otherwise.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Cept hang in there my friend, as things do eventually get better.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> <BR><B><I>"The PRE10DR"</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||12-20-2001||06:16 PM||||I have no first hand experience with depression so I have no clue what you are going through, but...<P>I have a very dear friend that suffers from rapid cycling depression. I've helped her through her worst episodes and now they are almost completely gone.<P>What seems to help is that you need to realize that the depression itself is caused by a chemical inbalance in your body. What I mean to say is that your life doesn't suck - you just perceive it that way. I mean, you take drugs and you feel better. The drugs don't make your life any better, it just makes you realize things aren't as bad as you think they are.<P>So first of all, keep that in mind - <B>always</B>. Be aware that no matter how bad you feel, that feeling is for 90% caused by that chemical inbalance. The enemy is something inside of you, not what happens out there. This enemy can be fought, and you can win. Don't give up.<P>Second, you need to realize that happiness isn't just about having friends or spending time with others. You could sit around the house all day long doing nothing, and still be perfectly happy. You could be up to your neck in debts - and still be happy. Don't let society tell you what's good or bad for you. The reason why there are so many suicides around Christmas is because commerce is telling you you should be with others right now, preferably spending an arm and a leg on gifts. Why is it ok to be alone the rest of the year, but not on Christmas? Let's be honest. There <B>is</B> no Santa Claus and most people don't even know what Christmas really is about. "tis the season to be jolly" - excuse me? How about the rest of the year? Why can't I feel like crap now and be "jolly" in the summer? Can I please decide for myself when I should feel happy? In short, don't let the time of year get to you.<P>I'm not sure if I'm helping you here, but all I'm trying to say is that this is just one moment in your life. There will be many more. Sometimes you'll feel good, sometimes you'll feel bad. And that's ok. One thing will never change though. There are <B>always</B> people out there that love you. People who derive part of their happiness just because <B>you</B> exist. They may not be around you all the time. You may not speak with them for years. But they do love you. Remember that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||12-20-2001||06:33 PM||||PLEASE give things some more time to turn around.  Depression will make you feel the way you do now, not allowing you to perceive things accurately.<P>Paxil is ONE fine anti-depressant, but not necessarily for everyone.  If you have intolerable side effects, there are plenty of other fine ones such as zoloft, prozac, celexa, wellbutrin, serzone, effexor, etc.<P>However, any of them take 2-3 weeks to take effect because of the way they work in the system, so you have to give it more time.<P>Also, now that you have started the paxil (and even if you hadn't), you need to see a doctor to work with you on this.  This isn't something you should be taking on alone.  <P>Once you start feeling better, THEN you can start focusing on rebuilding your life ||||reg||1
Z||000006||frontal lobe||12-20-2001||06:36 PM||||PLEASE give things some more time to turn around.  Depression will make you feel the way you do now, not allowing you to perceive things accurately.<P>Paxil is ONE fine anti-depressant, but not necessarily for everyone.  If you have intolerable side effects, there are plenty of other fine ones such as zoloft, prozac, celexa, wellbutrin, serzone, effexor, etc.<P>However, any of them take 2-3 weeks to take effect because of the way they work in the system, so you have to give it more time.<P>Also, now that you have started the paxil (and even if you hadn't), you need to see a doctor to work with you on this.  This isn't something you should be taking on alone.  <P>Once you start feeling better, THEN you can start focusing on rebuilding your life.  BE PATIENT.<P>Wishing you the best.||||reg||1
Z||000007||blue000||12-20-2001||07:44 PM||||Thank you all for your responce, today has been a very hard day, the mood has gone from one extreme to the other. It feels really great knowing that I can count on you guy's for support.<P>Thanks<P>JSocha the reference to judgement was not meant to bring back anything from another discussion, I just don't want to deal with any additional negativity today. I thank you for understanding.<P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000008||CoolBlue87GT||12-20-2001||08:19 PM||||Blue000,<P>Hey, I've been there, got my tee shirt.<P>It's tough, but survivable.<P>You have a great circle of friend here.<P>Hang in there<P>Take care||||reg||1
Z||000009||JSocha||12-21-2001||11:04 AM||||Blue000/funguy, you best be posting my friend at least everyother day or so, so we know you're still with us man!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>About the judgement thingy.  I didn't know what really to say when I posted.  And it was just my way of saying I'm not hear to judge you.  <I>Not my job.</I>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I was concerned at some point based on the last thread that that may have come across, because at times it did or may have gotten judgemental.<P>So what do you say?  Keep us posted every now and then my friend?  So at least we know you're okay?  With us through the Holidays?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> OH! And of course beyond!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000010||fierobaby||12-21-2001||11:13 AM||||Blue,<BR>I know its tough I have friend who have been through similar situations. Just know that you can always talk to us and we will listen... or in this case read... keep your head high we know you can do it!<P>Corinne<P>------------------<BR> <FONT COLOR="LETTER">  <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/fish.gif">  AIM: Fierobaby87  <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/fish.gif"> <BR>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero </FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierobaby-xmas-sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||12-21-2001||12:34 PM||||Hey brother looks like your not alone. I have also had my fair share of what you described, awkward don't know where to start and even if you do know where to start you worry about weather or not your doing it right. then just figure aww screw it and then it gets worse! <P><BR>1 Best thing you can do is TALK to someone,  this forum is good but you need the face to face understanding. once you have established a good person to share it with that is non critical of you. If they are critical of you the stop seeing them for a while until you recover. <P>2 Get a notebook and write down your problems then read them back to yourself. <P>3 Then write down 3 goals and keep them to yourself! something like "quit smoking"<BR>"get a job" ect.<P>4 Repeat the goals you have in mind as if you already have attained them " I AM a nonsmoker" or "I make 35,000 a year"  " I AM<BR>a computer programmer"<P>5 Stop using first person negitives in your thoughts as much as possible instead use the positive I AM, I HAVE, I MAKE. eg. "I AM Successfull"  instead of "I AM a failure"<P>( PLEASE people that don't understand this next one DON'T be critical)<P>6 probably the strangest of all is to do the following. Trust me and do it anyway. Look into a mirror and say " I like myself"  you will be surprised how tough it can really be. If you do it enough you will feel different almost instantly. <BR>I don't care what ANYONE says! It is OK to like yourself, nobody else really does and if they do, how do you know they aren't BSing you.<P>7 TAKE RESPONSIBILITY!<BR>  blame is the easiest thing to do! don't blame yourself and don't blame others for thing that happen in your life. Instead of blame say to yourself I AM RESPONSIBLE! you may have to say that through gritted teeth  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> but again trust me it will help and quickly. When you take responsibility for your actions you quickly develop ways to avoid trouble. THAT is not to say you wont make mistakes! Mistakes are what make us human, taking responsibility for mistakes makes us better humans (and rare ones at that)<P>In fact when people start talking about their problems and how it is someone elses fault, the quickest way to shut them up is to say "Your a responsible person and I'm sure you will be ok" It is almot a gaurentee they will shoot you a  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> look. or they will just get mad and say "what does that mean? nah ah it's not my fault"<P>The more you believe in yourself and the more you believe positively the more it will happen. <BR>Same goes for the negative stuff, the more you believe you are a failure the more you WILL BE a failure. <P>Just some history about myself and how lost I was<P>My self esteem was so low that when I walked down the street or the isle of a store I would not look up. I never looked people in the face when I spoke to them. I was a failure at everything I ever did so I didn't bother to do anything I rarely left the house and when I did it was to go get stoned. at 18 I was in really bad shape and this was to be the start of my adult life?<P>I changed it after someone gave me this advice and it worked. Oh I do still get depressed at times but it does not last more than 1 or 2 days.<P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000012||JSocha||12-21-2001||12:41 PM||||That was very well said or actually put Bill!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif"> <P>EDIT:  Fixed IMAGE Links.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Have to go find a new light bulb...mine burnt out...anybody willing to help me change it?<p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000013||Formula88||12-21-2001||12:52 PM||||Bill's got some good advice there.  I've had my bouts with depression, too.  The single biggest turning point for me was "taking responsibility" for my problems.  It's not assigning blame or fault, but once you realize you own your problems, you also realize you have the power to fix what is wrong.  It is within you.<P>The first steps will be difficult, <BR>possibly even painful,<BR>but after that, <BR>everthing else is real progress.<BR>-Ben Stein||||reg||1
Z||000014||84Bill||12-21-2001||02:20 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>  The single biggest turning point for me was "taking responsibility" for my problems.  It's not assigning blame or fault, but once you realize you own your problems, you also realize you have the power to fix what is wrong.  It is within you.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup when you blame others you make them "Responsible" for your problems and it empowers them to control you because you canot control what others do. If you take responsibilty you empower yourself! and the key is to be in control of yourself.<P>Everyone at one time or another falls into some kind of depression for one reason or another. For some there is a real chemical imbalance BUT if the drugs wont work there MUST be another reason for the depression. <P>I have found that drugs only make you "not care" about your problems. The problem with that is the drugs wear off and the problem is still there! So you become dependant on useless drugs that essentualy do nothing to fix the REAL problem. It is important to get some help in finding the root problem and fixing it. Now you can pay 100 bucks an hour to talk to a shrink or you can do 1 or 2 of the above post and get yourself out of it.<P>I for the most part write down my problems and the funny thing is that I find myself changing it because I'm like " is that whats bothering me?" and I write it down again and again I think " is that all it is?" For some reason when I write it down it seems insignificant and easly fixable.<P>Sometimes I will induce short term depression on purpose just for the heck of it (it is very easy to get depressed) and write some poetry or to understand (empathize) how someone feels for whatever reason. <P>Blue for example, he has gone through much lately and he feels out of control. He made some very stressfull decisions the in themselves will cause depression automaticly. Moving is the #1 stress causer even if it's just across town and long term stress will lead to depression. The job didn't work out and he feels he has failed and is a failure  but the fact that he gave it a go does not a failure make. I must commend him for trying. Most will never attempt it because it's hard to pack up and move to another city. I'll bet his back is hurting and his stomach is upset to boot. this is a physical manifestation of "I can't but I have to"  Or the classic I cant get a job but I know I have to stress<P>Eddison tried 4000 times to make a lightbulb (no pun) before he finaly did. He was interviewed at about attempt 2000 and was loosing creadibility. The young interviewer asked Eddison " why do you insist on making a lightbulb? everyone knows that you are crazy for trying so many times and failing and are you afraid of being labled a failure and loosing credibility?  Eddison sat up and looked him dead in the eye and said "Son I HAVE found 2000 ways NOT to make a lightbulb and that puts me precisly 2000 ways closer to making one" Eddison did not view himself a failure even if he failed 2000 times!<P>yeah you can easily stand at home plate and feel that you will never hit a home run and get all depressed and give up before you do but the law of averages says that if you stand there long enough and swing enough times you will hit one over the fence eventually.<P>Another thing is "I'll try" if you say "I'll try" to make it to the party. Translation "I will make the effort but I will fail to make it to the party" Never say "I'll try to get a job" because you basicly have already determined you will not and therefor will not make a 100% effort. I have for the mostpart removed "I'll try" from my daily dialog and if you hear me say that it means 99% chance I will not.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Gawd my finners are going today!<P>OH AND ONE OTHER THING<BR>JSocha<BR>Q: How many shrinks does it take to chage a lightbulb?<BR>A: None it has to willing to change its self  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000015||blue000||12-21-2001||09:02 PM||||Well not much in the side of improvement, your words are a great source of inspiration. I actually got out of the house today and got myself a hair cut. I know that is not much to must, but for me that was the first time in 3 days out of the house. This is going to take some time I see, I hope I have the strenght to see my way past it. <P><BR>Thanks again for your words ||||reg||9
Z||000016||stimpy||12-21-2001||10:50 PM||||Brother, I know your pain. I medicated myself with Marijuana for many many years. It has it's way of masking pain. So I pass no judgement there. But I found that the time of my life that I was stoned was wasted time, and now at 34 I am doing my best to keep up with people 10 years younger then I in the career field. I don't have any desire to start using again and putting myself back in that limbo.<P>As far as the anti-depressant medications go, I found that Wellbutrin has the best effect for me. The problem is, without insurance, getting the help you need can be very rough. Don't let that stop you from trying, though. Check your yellow pages for county mental health clinics. It will mean dealing with some real crazys in the waiting rooms, but you will get treatment and therapy that way.<P>I applaud that you were able to get your ass outside today. I respect what a challenge that can be in your condition. Try to do it more often. Get out for a walk every day. The exercise and change of scenery is the cheapest medicine you can get. <P>Also try out writing in a journal. Sometimes it is hard to really realize why you are depressed, and if you just pour your thoughts out, you will be surprised of the things that come out of it.<P>And please, keep posting in this thread. I'll be checking it and I'm sure a lot of other good people will be, too. And I personally promise to kick cyber-ass if anyone decides to be "judgemental". This is a safe thread, bro.||||reg||1
Z||000017||JSocha||12-22-2001||12:16 AM||||yeah...ummmmm?.....what Stimpy said.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000018||yo_sweets_babe||12-22-2001||12:47 AM||||hey, way to get out of the house, that was really awesome of u.<P>i've had my share of depression already and i'm not even out of highschool yet.  i went through depression and tried to commit suicide, but i'm SO HAPPY it didn't work. u may not believe it, but i understand everything you're going through and feeling.  <P>journals are awesome inspiration.  when you're feeling better about everything, itz really interesting to look back on how much progress you've made.<P>talking to a professional does help a lot, even if u don't gain anything, itz still worth a try.  you already made a big step when u first came to the people here, because they know what they're talkin about and won't judge.<P>good luck, take care, and hang in there.  we're all behind ya!<P><BR>"Turn that frown upside down because you never know who could be falling in love with your smile."||||reg||7
Z||000019||fogglethorpe||12-22-2001||08:39 AM||||Blue,<BR>There are two kinds of people...those who don't have it all together, and those who are good at pretending they do. I appreciate that you would pour out your heart like this. <BR>I am familiar with depression, because I have struggled with it myself, although it appears that yours may be more severe than mine. <BR>During my most serious bouts, I couldn't get myself motivated to go to work, get out of bed, or make the simplest decisions.<BR>There were two things that gave me strength (besides counselling, etc.)<BR>One was the power of positive confession. I found some truths (in my case, it was biblical scripture...in your case, it could be whatever works for you). For example, "God has not given us the spirit of fear, but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind." or "Cast all your cares uon him, because he cares for you."<BR>The second thing is that the right music for a situation can have enormous power.<BR>"I've seen the darkness<BR>and it saw me<BR>but here in the light<BR>where the dark can't see<BR>there is a sweet glow of mercy<BR>that covers me<P>Now I am found<BR>but once was lost<BR>and there in the gutter<BR>where the wheels came off<BR>there was a sweet glow of mercy<BR>that covered me"       -Gary Chapman<P>I know this doesn't "make it all better", but maybe in it you'll find strength for the moment. God bless.||||reg||1
Z||000020||blue000||12-22-2001||12:49 PM||||Hello again all<P>Today is another blue day, it is sunny here and most people would say that is a perfect day. Mornings are the worst, it's the start of a long dark day in my life once again. I feel restless, depress with high anxiety. I have to force myself to eat, I have to get out of bed early and start watching TV so I can stop thinking, my worst enemy is my mind. I cry for no reason, I have no energy, I'm so glad that I can vent here with out any one knowing who I am. My wife in Chicago doesn't know the hell I'm putting my self tru, and I can't share it with her as it pushes her away. I will be going to Chicago on the 26th and come back on the 3rd, I have very mixed emotions about it, but I feel I have to do it for myself. I miss her a great deal, but I will have to hide my feelings, I hope I can feel better before I go. I know that my thoughts are all over the place, that is kind of how I feel. the days are so long, I can't wait till the night so I know that the end is near. I wish I could go to sleep and not wake, such a relief. <P>Thanks for the space to vent, and the great words of wisdom. Pardon the spelling.||||reg||9
Z||000021||FieroKyle||12-22-2001||02:52 PM||||Well, reading through all this, I can't really say anything more because its already been said. Haven't been in this situation myself.<P>I am just happy to see that people came in here and took it serious with no flaming, judgement, pointing fingers, or name calling. Sometimes on what see here it gets ridduculous. Good job everyone.<P>Blue, hope ya make it through this. Try to find a way out that suites you best and take it. Whether its taking meds, going to see help, being with family, getting a job ect.... Keep yourself busy anyways. Don't let the holidays get to ya, its just another day like Cliff said. Good luck, keep checking in.<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokylesig_1.gif">      <P>My Fast 'n Black Car<BR>Kyle - 85' GT||||reg||1
Z||000022||JSocha||12-22-2001||06:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroKyle:<BR><B>I am just happy to see that people came in here and took it serious with no flaming, judgement, pointing fingers, or name calling. Sometimes on what see here it gets ridduculous. Good job everyone.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> As a group, I feel we are all pretty much a mature bunch even though we bicker every now and again, don't you agree?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> BTW, anybody here seen my diapers?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000023||00lE||12-23-2001||07:08 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by blue000:<BR><B> I found the side effects to <BR>much and a friend recommended pot as an alternative, and for 1 1/2 <BR>years it worked pretty good with low incidents of depression or <BR>anxiety. I meet the love of my life last Christmas and in June we <BR>married. I then quit my job and we relocated to Chicago. Due to the <BR>need to pass a drug test, I stopped using pot and didn't use anything <BR>for depression.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have heard that marijuana has different effects on people, depending on their metabolism.  Perhaps the chemical imbalance causing your depression was at least partially corrected by smoking pot?  If it worked before, perhaps it still will.  Might at least be worth a test to find out.<P>I do not advocate drug abuse for any reason, and as for myself, I do not drink alcohol nor do I take any illegal drug, but marijuana has proven to be medically valuable for treatment of various other illnesses such as glaucoma.  Perhaps it could be "the perfect anti-depressant" for those with a certain metabolism and brain chemistry imbalance?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||stimpy||12-23-2001||09:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>  I have heard that marijuana has different effects on people, depending on their metabolism.  Perhaps the chemical imbalance causing your depression was at least partially corrected by smoking pot?  If it worked before, perhaps it still will.  Might at least be worth a test to find out.<P>I do not advocate drug abuse for any reason, and as for myself, I do not drink alcohol nor do I take any illegal drug, but marijuana has proven to be medically valuable for treatment of various other illnesses such as glaucoma.  Perhaps it could be "the perfect anti-depressant" for those with a certain metabolism and brain chemistry imbalance?<P><BR>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-23-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Tread lightly there. I would highly NOT recommend turning back to pot in order to self medicate. While it may lighten some of the symptoms, it will (in my opinion & experience)actually magnify the causes of the depression. Here's why.<P>1. Dope is expensive. Here is a guy that is having troubles getting employment, I doubt that he has the disposable income to buy dope. Example. My pot habit cost me about $250 a month. My Wellbutrin prescription cost $90 a month. Find the value there.<P>2. Dope is illegal. I'm not moralizing here, but I don't think that when you are in a delicate mental state, you need the pressure of the potential of getting busted. And that potential is always there.<P>3. Dope has a social stigma attached. The majority of society looks at users of marijuana as a sub-culture. Employers utilise drug screening to remove users from potential applicants for most jobs. People who do not use generally do not pick out users to establish friendships with. And as for all those fun pot-smoking pals you had before, where are they now. They see you as a buzz-killer now, most likely.<P>Even though you don't have insurance, bite the bullet, go see the doctor and get yourself on some good medication. If Paxil does the trick, great.  Wellbutrin works wonders for me. (Paxil just freaked me the **** out!)||||reg||1
Z||000025||Indy-2M4||12-23-2001||09:52 AM||||Blue....I really feel for you as well as the rest. Just 2 years ago at this time of year I went through a divorce and It really ****ed me up. The doctors put me on Celexa and it really, really does wonders. I still have bouts with depression, hence the doctors chose to keep me on Celexa. You have a very strong circle of friends here for you. We may not hold all the answers, but getting your feelings out and having friends to listen can do wonders. I will keep you in my prayers. Just hang in there....you WILL get through it! God bless you!<P>Kathy||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||12-23-2001||12:12 PM||||Speaking from my experience in working in a psyche hospital, I did learn a few things about anti-depressants.<P>I would like to state my displeasure at your doctor(s) who gave you a high-powered prescription, didn't tell you how to use it, and didn't follow up. That's  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><P>find a doctor that is willing to follow-up, and one that explains fully how the stuff works, it's shortcomings, and any potential side-effects. In the previous case, there should have been a followup appointment to ensure that it was working. if it wasn't something should have been done.<P>If you can, I would go see another doctor. You CAN beat it. The advice here has been mostly great. Follow what you can, and work up the strength to go see somebody else.<P>You can do it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000027||blue000||12-23-2001||10:38 PM||||Thank you guy's. this is still very hard, but I'm taking responsibility of my future one day at the time, I will be going to visit my wife in Chicago on the 26th and spend new years with her. I have mix emotions on this but I have to see how things are and if there is anything to work with. My wife didn't realize the severity of my depression, I had talked to her a lot about it but I guess she didn't really pay to much attention. I hope that now with meds and I hope soon with insurance things will get better for me. I will keep you all posted every day. Thank you for the space available Cliff, this is a real help for me. ||||reg||9
Z||000028||JSocha||12-24-2001||02:23 AM||||Hey Blue, just would like to wish you good luck in Chicago in the coming week.  Your PFF family will be here for you to listen and of course let you unload some baggage, if need be.<P>Merry Christmas and Happy New Year my friend.  May it be safe and joyous so you can once again be with us after the Holidays, sharing any insight on Fiero's, as well as any wisdom  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> and any humor you may or may not have been posting under your alternate Fiero Moniker which although may remain a mystery to us, but more then likely is shared and welcomed by the rest of us in this community as being one of our close friends.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Hate to loose yah!<P>Take care...and you best post to let us know how your doing! Yah hear?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000029||blue000||12-24-2001||09:04 PM||||Christmas just plain sucks, can't wait till is over. I just had to vent. Spending Christmas alone just plain sucks.<P>||||reg||8
Z||000030||JSocha||12-24-2001||10:38 PM||||Blue, you're not alone this Christmas Eve.  Believe it or not, you're actually here with some of us.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000031||Joe Torma||12-24-2001||11:14 PM||||Yeah, your definetley not alone at Pennocks....or in depression either.<P>I used to be on Paxil years ago.  I'm not in the best state of mind at this time either.<P>I just keep my mind off of things by doing something creative, or just plain anything that keeps my mind off of stuff(like posting here often).<P>Its not easy, I know.<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 12-24-2001).]||||reg||1
